window.AIBEMOCK={"n":2,"title":"Mock Test 2","theme":"Full syllabus","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"A second full-length paper with the same subject-wise mark distribution, using fresh questions. Take it a few days after Mock Test 1 to see what your revision has fixed.","minutes":210,"neg":0,"pass":{"General / OBC":45,"SC / ST / PwD":40},"asOn":"19 November 2026","q":[{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"In Pramatha Nath Mullick v Pradyumna Kumar Mullick (1925), the Privy Council held that a Hindu idol is","o":["a mere object of worship, incapable of owning property","a charitable trust and not a person in law","a juristic person, which acts through its shebait","a minor, represented by a guardian under the Guardians and Wards Act"],"a":2,"e":"A Hindu idol is a juristic person with the power to own property and sue through its shebait or manager. The shebait is not the owner of the idol's property."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 193(3)(ii) of the BNSS, the police must inform the informant or victim of the progress of the investigation within","o":["ninety days, including by electronic means","one hundred and twenty days, including by electronic means","thirty days, including by electronic means","sixty days, including by electronic means"],"a":0,"e":"This victim-centric provision requires police to communicate the progress of investigation within 90 days, including by electronic communication. The police report must also be accompanied by documents and statements relied on."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code, 2020, a woman worker may be employed at night (before 6 a.m. or after 7 p.m.) only with","o":["her consent, and subject to prescribed conditions for safety","the permission of the Labour Commissioner in every single case of night work","the written consent of her husband or her guardian, as the case may be","no consent and no conditions, as there is no restriction on night work at all"],"a":0,"e":"The Code permits women to work in all establishments and at night, but only with their consent and subject to safety and other conditions prescribed by the appropriate Government."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"For the offence of criminal conspiracy to commit an offence under Section 61(2) of the BNS, an overt act in pursuance of the agreement is","o":["required only if the offence agreed upon is punishable with death","essential in every case, even where the object of the agreement is an offence","not required, as the agreement itself is the gist of the offence","required, along with the actual commission of the offence agreed upon"],"a":2,"e":"Mere agreement between two or more persons to commit an illegal act, or a legal act by illegal means, is an offence. An overt act is required only for conspiracies to commit acts that are not themselves offences."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In Prem Shankar Shukla v Delhi Administration (1980), the Supreme Court held that handcuffing of prisoners","o":["is mandatory for every person accused of a non-bailable offence, as a rule","is permitted for any accused person at the sole discretion of the escorting officer","is a normal incident of every lawful arrest made by the police, in every case","is prima facie inhuman and unreasonable, and allowed only in extreme circumstances"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that routine handcuffing violates Articles 14, 19 and 21. It is permissible only where there is a clear and present danger of escape, and the reasons must be recorded. BNSS Section 43 now lists the categories of offenders for whom handcuffs may be used."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Sunni law, a Muslim can bequeath by will, without the consent of his heirs, not more than","o":["one-fourth of the net estate after funeral expenses and debts","one-third of the net estate after funeral expenses and debts","one-half of the net estate after funeral expenses and debts","two-thirds of the net estate after funeral expenses and debts"],"a":1,"e":"A bequest beyond one-third requires the consent of the heirs, given after the testator's death. A bequest to an heir also requires the consent of the other heirs."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 30 of the Advocates Act, 1961, an advocate whose name is entered on any State roll is entitled to practise","o":["only in the courts of the State on whose roll his name is entered, and not elsewhere","in all courts across India, but not before any tribunal or evidence-taking authority at all","in all courts, before tribunals, and before any person legally authorised to take evidence","only in the High Court of his enrolment and in the courts subordinate to that High Court"],"a":2,"e":"Section 30 gives advocates a right to practise throughout India in the Supreme Court, all courts and tribunals, and before any person legally authorised to take evidence. The provision was brought into force only in 2011."},{"s":"Limitation Law","q":"Under Article 65 of the Limitation Act, 1963, the limitation period for a suit for possession of immovable property based on title is","o":["thirty years from when the defendant's possession becomes adverse","twelve years from when the defendant's possession becomes adverse","six years from when the defendant's possession becomes adverse","three years from when the defendant's possession becomes adverse"],"a":1,"e":"The period is 12 years from when the defendant's possession becomes adverse to the plaintiff. Thirty years applies for suits by the Government. Adverse possession for the statutory period extinguishes the owner's right under Section 27."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"The principle that an arbitral tribunal can rule on its own jurisdiction, as under Section 16 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, is known as","o":["lex arbitri","res judicata","ex aequo et bono","kompetenz-kompetenz"],"a":3,"e":"Section 16 empowers the tribunal to rule on its own jurisdiction, including on objections about the existence or validity of the arbitration agreement. Lex arbitri refers to the law of the seat, and ex aequo et bono to deciding according to equity and good conscience."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"An application to set aside an ex parte decree under Order IX Rule 13 CPC must be filed within","o":["thirty days from the date of the decree","fifteen days from the date of the decree","ninety days from the date of the decree","sixty days from the date of the decree"],"a":0,"e":"Article 123 of the Limitation Act, 1963 prescribes 30 days from the date of the decree, or from the date of knowledge where summons was not duly served. The defendant must show sufficient cause for non-appearance."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"In Overseas Tankship (UK) Ltd. v Morts Dock (Wagon Mound No. 1) (1961), the Privy Council held that damages in negligence are recoverable only for consequences that are","o":["intended by the defendant at the time of the act","reasonably foreseeable","direct, however unforeseeable they may be","limited to physical injury to the claimant"],"a":1,"e":"The Court rejected the 'directness' test in Re Polemis and held that the test of remoteness in negligence is reasonable foreseeability of the kind of damage."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"In which of the following situations does the right of private defence of the body NOT extend to voluntarily causing death?","o":["An assault made with the intention of causing simple hurt","An assault made with the intention of committing rape","An assault causing reasonable apprehension of grievous hurt","An assault made with the intention of kidnapping or abducting"],"a":0,"e":"Under Section 38 BNS (earlier Section 100 IPC), the right extends to causing death if the assault gives rise to a reasonable apprehension of death or grievous hurt, or is with intent to commit rape, unnatural lust, kidnapping, abduction, wrongful confinement or an acid attack. Simple hurt does not qualify."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"Under the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981, no person may establish any industrial plant in an air pollution control area without","o":["the permission of the Central Pollution Control Board alone","the previous consent of the State Pollution Control Board","a licence granted by the District Magistrate","a no-objection certificate from the Gram Panchayat"],"a":1,"e":"Section 21 requires the previous consent of the State Board to establish or operate an industrial plant in an air pollution control area. The Board may grant or refuse consent, subject to conditions."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Where organised crime under Section 111 of the BNS results in the death of a person, the minimum fine that must be imposed is","o":["Rs. 10 lakh","Rs. 1 lakh","Rs. 5 lakh","Rs. 25 lakh"],"a":0,"e":"Section 111(2)(a) provides death or life imprisonment and a fine not less than Rs. 10 lakh where the organised crime results in death. In other cases, the punishment is imprisonment of not less than five years, extendable to life, and a fine of not less than Rs. 5 lakh."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Section 43 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882 embodies the doctrine of","o":["feeding the grant by estoppel","part performance of a contract by the transferee under Section 53A","election, the doctrine that one must choose between inconsistent rights","lis pendens, the doctrine of transfer during the pendency of a suit"],"a":0,"e":"If a person fraudulently or erroneously represents that he is authorised to transfer property and professes to transfer it for consideration, the transferee may, at his option, claim any interest the transferor later acquires in the property during subsistence of the contract."},{"s":"Environmental Law","q":"The public trust doctrine, under which the State holds natural resources as a trustee for the public, was applied in India in","o":["T.N. Godavarman Thirumulpad v Union of India (1997)","M.C. Mehta v Kamal Nath (1997)","Subhash Kumar v State of Bihar (1991)","Indian Council for Enviro-Legal Action v Union of India (1996)"],"a":1,"e":"In Kamal Nath, a motel was built on the bed of the Beas river. The Supreme Court held that natural resources such as rivers, forests and the air are held by the State as a trustee for public use and cannot be converted into private ownership."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Re-examination of a witness must be directed to","o":["the explanation of matters referred to in cross-examination","bringing out entirely new matter without the court's leave","contradicting the witness's earlier answers on all points","confronting the witness with documents not produced earlier"],"a":0,"e":"Re-examination is limited to explaining matters referred to in cross-examination. New matter can be introduced only with the court's permission, and the adverse party may then cross-examine on it."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"The 'rarest of rare' doctrine for imposing the death penalty was laid down in which decision?","o":["Jagmohan Singh v State of Uttar Pradesh (1973)","Rajendra Prasad v State of Uttar Pradesh (1979)","Mithu v State of Punjab (1983)","Bachan Singh v State of Punjab (1980)"],"a":3,"e":"Bachan Singh upheld the constitutional validity of the death penalty but held that it should be imposed only in the rarest of rare cases, after weighing aggravating and mitigating circumstances. Mithu struck down the mandatory death penalty under Section 303 IPC."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"In a usufructuary mortgage under Section 58(d) of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, the mortgagee","o":["may foreclose the mortgage after giving due notice to the mortgagor","holds only the title deeds of the mortgaged property as security","is given possession and retains the rents and profits in lieu of interest","may institute a suit for the sale of the mortgaged property upon default"],"a":2,"e":"The mortgagee is delivered possession and entitled to appropriate rents and profits towards interest, or principal, or both. There is no personal covenant to pay, so he cannot sue for sale or foreclosure."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"As per the guidelines in D.K. Basu v State of West Bengal (1997), an arrestee should be subjected to medical examination by a trained doctor during custody every","o":["twenty-four hours","seven days","forty-eight hours","seventy-two hours"],"a":2,"e":"The D.K. Basu guidelines also require a memo of arrest attested by a witness, intimation to a relative or friend, entry of the arrest in a diary, and allowing the arrestee to meet his lawyer during interrogation."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"In Patil Automation Pvt. Ltd. v Rakheja Engineers Pvt. Ltd. (2022), the Supreme Court held that pre-institution mediation under Section 12A of the Commercial Courts Act, 2015 is","o":["applicable only to commercial suits above Rs. 10 crore in value","applicable only to disputes that are covered by an arbitration clause","directory, and may be dispensed with entirely at the plaintiff's option","mandatory, and a plaint filed without it is liable to rejection"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that Section 12A is mandatory unless urgent interim relief is sought, and a suit instituted without compliance is liable to be rejected under Order VII Rule 11. The ruling was applied prospectively from 20 August 2022."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"The American Realist view that law is a prediction of what the courts will do in fact is associated with","o":["Oliver Wendell Holmes","Jeremy Bentham","Ronald Dworkin","Friedrich Carl von Savigny"],"a":0,"e":"Holmes, in 'The Path of the Law', said that the prophecies of what the courts will do in fact are what he means by law. Savigny founded the Historical school, Bentham was a utilitarian, and Dworkin argued for law as integrity."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which decision of the Supreme Court held that the opinion of the Chief Justice of India has primacy in the appointment of Supreme Court judges and must reflect the collective view of senior judges?","o":["Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v Union of India (1993)","Union of India v Sankalchand Himatlal Sheth (1977), on judicial transfers","Shamsher Singh v State of Punjab (1974), on the Governor's powers","S.P. Gupta v Union of India (1981), the First Judges case"],"a":0,"e":"The Second Judges case overruled S.P. Gupta, which had given primacy to the Executive. It held that 'consultation' means concurrence, and that the CJI's recommendation reflects the collective view of the Collegium. The Collegium was later expanded to five judges in the 1998 Presidential Reference."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 20 CPC, a suit may be instituted in a court within whose local limits","o":["only the plaintiff resides, irrespective of any other facts of the case","the cause of action arises wholly, and not merely in part, within those limits","the defendant's counsel has his office or chambers, whatever the facts may be","the defendant resides or carries on business, or the cause of action wholly or partly arises"],"a":3,"e":"Section 20 is the residuary provision on territorial jurisdiction. The cause of action may arise wholly or in part. The residence of the plaintiff alone does not confer jurisdiction."},{"s":"Tort & Consumer Law","q":"Under Section 41 of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, an appeal against an order of the District Commission lies to the State Commission within","o":["ninety days","forty-five days","sixty days","thirty days"],"a":1,"e":"The appeal must be filed within 45 days, extendable for sufficient cause. The appellant must deposit fifty per cent of the amount ordered by the District Commission."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Selvi v State of Karnataka (2010), the Supreme Court held that the involuntary administration of narco-analysis, polygraph and brain-mapping tests violates","o":["Article 19(1)(a) alone, which protects freedom of expression","Article 22(1) alone, which protects the right to a lawyer","Article 20(1) alone, which protects against ex post facto laws","Article 20(3) and the personal liberty protected by Article 21"],"a":3,"e":"Compulsory administration of these techniques violates the right against self-incrimination under Article 20(3) and the right to privacy and personal liberty under Article 21. Voluntary administration is permitted subject to safeguards."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"The principle of non-refoulement is contained in Article 33 of the","o":["1951 Convention relating to the Status of Refugees","Charter of the United Nations, 1945, on use of force","Convention on the High Seas, 1958, on the freedom of navigation","Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, 1969, on treaty rules"],"a":0,"e":"Article 33 prohibits the expulsion or return of a refugee to territories where his life or freedom would be threatened. India is not a party to the 1951 Convention, though the principle is argued to be a part of customary international law."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"The effect of Section 10 CPC (res sub judice) on a subsequently instituted suit is that the court shall","o":["dismiss the subsequent suit as barred by law","refuse to register the subsequent suit at the outset","stay the subsequent suit and not proceed with its trial","transfer the subsequent suit to the earlier court automatically"],"a":2,"e":"Section 10 bars the trial, not the institution, of a later suit on the same matter between the same parties. The suit is stayed pending decision of the earlier suit. A decree passed in violation of Section 10 is not a nullity."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Under the Information Technology Act, 2000, a licence to operate as a Certifying Authority is granted by","o":["the Ministry of Home Affairs","the Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT-In)","the Controller of Certifying Authorities","the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India"],"a":2,"e":"The Controller of Certifying Authorities, appointed under Section 17, licenses and regulates Certifying Authorities who issue digital signature certificates."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Indra Sawhney v Union of India (1992), the Supreme Court held that reservations under Article 16(4) should ordinarily not exceed","o":["thirty-three per cent of the posts","forty per cent of the posts","sixty per cent of the posts","fifty per cent of the posts"],"a":3,"e":"The nine-judge Bench upheld 27% reservation for OBCs, but held that the total should ordinarily stay within 50%, that the 'creamy layer' be excluded, and that reservation in promotions is not permissible. In Janhit Abhiyan v Union of India (2022), the Court upheld EWS reservation, which goes beyond that ceiling."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Code on Wages, 2019, the components that are excluded from the definition of 'wages' cannot exceed what proportion of the total remuneration?","o":["thirty per cent","forty per cent","sixty per cent","fifty per cent"],"a":3,"e":"If the excluded components, such as bonus, house rent allowance or conveyance allowance, exceed 50% of total remuneration, the excess is added back to wages. This affects calculation of gratuity, PF and retrenchment compensation."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"In a Sessions case, Section 250 of the BNSS requires the accused to file an application for discharge within","o":["sixty days from the date of committal of the case","fifteen days from the date of committal of the case","ninety days from the date of committal of the case","thirty days from the date of committal of the case"],"a":0,"e":"Section 250 sets a time limit of 60 days from committal for the discharge application, which was not provided for under the CrPC. The Judge must also frame the charge within sixty days from the first hearing on charge."},{"s":"Cyber Law","q":"Which Section of the Information Technology Act, 2000 punishes cyber terrorism, with imprisonment which may extend to imprisonment for life?","o":["Section 66B","Section 66D","Section 66E","Section 66F"],"a":3,"e":"Section 66F covers cyber terrorism. Section 66B punishes dishonestly receiving a stolen computer resource, 66D cheating by personation, and 66E violation of privacy."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under the first proviso to Section 480(1) of the BNSS, a court may release on bail even a person accused of an offence punishable with death or life imprisonment, if he is","o":["in custody for more than fifteen days","under sixteen years of age","above sixty-five years of age","a resident of the same district as the complainant"],"a":1,"e":"The proviso allows bail for a person under sixteen, a woman, or a sick or infirm person, even where the offence is punishable with death or life imprisonment. Bail cannot be granted in such cases where there are reasonable grounds for believing the person guilty, except under the proviso."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Which of the following is a ground for exercising revisional jurisdiction under Section 115 CPC?","o":["Exercise of a jurisdiction not vested in the court by law","A wrong appreciation of the evidence by the trial court","A mistaken interpretation of a document by the trial court","A wrong finding of fact recorded by the trial court"],"a":0,"e":"Section 115 allows revision where the subordinate court exercised jurisdiction not vested in it, failed to exercise jurisdiction vested in it, or acted illegally or with material irregularity. Revision is not a means of reviewing facts or the evidence."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"In Pradeep Kumar Biswas v Indian Institute of Chemical Biology (2002), the Supreme Court held that a body is 'State' under Article 12 if it is","o":["funded mainly through donations received from members of the general public","financially, functionally and administratively dominated by or under the control of the Government","registered as a company under the Companies Act, whatever the extent of control","established under a statute enacted by Parliament or a State Legislature"],"a":1,"e":"The Court overruled Sukhdev Singh's approach and held that the question is whether the body is financially, functionally and administratively dominated by, or under the control of, the Government, so that the control is pervasive."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"In Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball Co. (1893), it was held that a general offer to the public","o":["is only an invitation to offer, which cannot be accepted by anyone","can be accepted only if the acceptance is notified to the offeror in writing","cannot be made to the world at large under the law of contract","is a valid offer, accepted by performing the conditions stated in it"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that the advertisement was an offer to the world, which anyone could accept by performing its conditions. Communication of acceptance was not needed, and the Rs. 1,000 deposit showed intention to create legal relations."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"Under Section 6 of the General Clauses Act, 1897, the effect of repeal of an enactment is that","o":["all proceedings pending under the repealed law automatically abate on repeal","any earlier law that was repealed is automatically revived by the repeal","rights and liabilities already accrued under the repealed law are preserved","penalties incurred under the repealed law are wiped out by the repeal"],"a":2,"e":"Unless a different intention appears, a repeal does not revive anything not in force at the time, or affect the previous operation of the enactment, rights, privileges, obligations, liabilities, penalties or pending legal proceedings under it."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 135 of the Companies Act, 2013, an eligible company must spend on corporate social responsibility at least what proportion of its average net profits of the three preceding financial years?","o":["five per cent","one per cent","two per cent","three per cent"],"a":2,"e":"CSR applies to companies with net worth of Rs. 500 crore or more, turnover of Rs. 1,000 crore or more, or net profit of Rs. 5 crore or more in the preceding financial year. They must spend at least 2% of the average net profits."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 36B of the Advocates Act, 1961, a disciplinary proceeding pending before a State Bar Council for more than one year from receipt of the complaint stands transferred to","o":["the Advocate General of the State, for inquiry","the High Court of the State, for disposal","the Registry of the Supreme Court, for inquiry","the Bar Council of India, for disposal"],"a":3,"e":"Section 36B requires the State Bar Council to dispose of a disciplinary matter within one year. If it does not, the proceeding stands transferred to the Bar Council of India, which may dispose of it as if it were its own."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Mitakshara law, severance of joint status of a Hindu family takes place from","o":["the date of registration of the partition deed before the Sub-Registrar","the date of division of the property by metes and bounds on the ground","the date of the court decree for partition, and not any earlier date","the date of the unequivocal declaration of intention to separate"],"a":3,"e":"In Girija Bai v Sadashiv Dhundiraj (1916), the Privy Council held that a clear, unequivocal declaration of intention to separate, communicated to the other members, is enough to bring about a severance of joint status. Division by metes and bounds is a later step."},{"s":"Jurisprudence","q":"In H.L.A. Hart's theory, the secondary rule that identifies the valid rules of a legal system is the","o":["rule of change","rule of obligation","rule of recognition","rule of adjudication"],"a":2,"e":"Hart's legal system is a union of primary rules (imposing duties) and secondary rules (recognition, change and adjudication). The rule of recognition specifies the criteria of validity."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"Under the Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013, the Lokpal consists of a Chairperson and not more than how many members?","o":["ten members","six members","eight members","four members"],"a":2,"e":"The Lokpal has a Chairperson and up to eight members, of whom 50% must be judicial members. At least 50% of the members are to be from SC, ST, OBC, minorities and women."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"The opinion given by the Supreme Court on a Presidential Reference under Article 143 is","o":["advisory in nature, and the Court is bound to answer every reference that is made","binding on all courts in India as law declared under Article 141 of the Constitution","enforceable as a judgment of the Court through contempt of court proceedings","advisory, and the Court may decline to answer the reference"],"a":3,"e":"Advisory opinions are not judgments and are not binding in the way a decision is, though they carry great persuasive weight. In the Ayodhya reference (1993), the Court declined to answer a question as it was unnecessary and tended to favour one religious community."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 35A CPC, compensatory costs for a false or vexatious claim or defence may not exceed","o":["Rs. 1,000","Rs. 3,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 5,000"],"a":1,"e":"Section 35A permits compensatory costs up to Rs. 3,000, or the pecuniary jurisdiction of the court, whichever is less. Several High Courts have amended the limit by their own State amendments."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Section 12(5) of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005, the Magistrate shall endeavour to dispose of an application within","o":["one hundred and twenty days from the date of its first hearing","ninety days from the date of its first hearing","thirty days from the date of its first hearing","sixty days from the date of its first hearing"],"a":3,"e":"Under Section 12(4), the first hearing must be fixed within three days of receipt of the application. The Magistrate must endeavour to dispose of it within sixty days of the first hearing."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Article 22(2) of the Constitution requires a person arrested to be produced before the nearest Magistrate within twenty-four hours, excluding","o":["the time taken by the police to complete the investigation of the offence","Sundays and public holidays that fall within the twenty-four hour period","the time necessary for the journey from the place of arrest to the court","the time spent on the medical examination of the person who has been arrested"],"a":2,"e":"The 24-hour period excludes only the time necessary for the journey from the place of arrest to the Magistrate's court. Section 58 BNSS (earlier Section 57 CrPC) is to the same effect."},{"s":"Public International Law","q":"Under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, 1982, the territorial sea of a coastal State extends up to","o":["twelve nautical miles from the baseline","twenty-four nautical miles from the baseline","three nautical miles from the baseline","two hundred nautical miles from the baseline"],"a":0,"e":"The territorial sea extends up to 12 nautical miles, the contiguous zone to 24 nautical miles, and the exclusive economic zone to 200 nautical miles. Foreign vessels have a right of innocent passage through the territorial sea."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 112 of the Evidence Act, birth of a child within how many days after dissolution of marriage, the mother remaining unmarried, is conclusive proof of legitimacy?","o":["three hundred and sixty-five days","two hundred and eighty days","one hundred and eighty days","two hundred and seventy days"],"a":1,"e":"Birth during a valid marriage, or within 280 days after its dissolution while the mother remains unmarried, is conclusive proof of legitimacy unless the parties had no access to each other at any time when the child could have been begotten."},{"s":"Alternative Dispute Resolution","q":"A domestic arbitral award is enforceable as a decree of the court under Section 36 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 once","o":["the award has been registered with the Registrar of the court concerned","the time to apply under Section 34 has expired, or the application has been refused","the arbitrator's fees have been paid in full by both of the parties","the Supreme Court has confirmed the award on an appeal by the parties"],"a":1,"e":"Section 36 allows enforcement after the three-month period (plus thirty days) under Section 34 expires, or the application is refused. Since the 2015 amendment, filing a Section 34 application does not automatically stay the award."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Which of the following taxes was NOT subsumed under the Goods and Services Tax?","o":["Central Excise Duty on the manufacture of goods","Value Added Tax levied by the States on sale of goods","Basic customs duty on imports","Service Tax on the provision of services"],"a":2,"e":"GST subsumed central excise, service tax, State VAT, entry tax, octroi, luxury tax and certain cesses. Basic customs duty continues, while IGST is levied on imports in addition to it."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 356 of the BNS, the punishment for defamation is","o":["rigorous imprisonment up to two years only, without any option of fine","simple imprisonment up to two years, or fine, or both, or community service","imprisonment up to three years along with a mandatory fine in all cases","simple imprisonment up to one year, or fine only, but no community service"],"a":1,"e":"Section 356(2) BNS corresponds to Section 500 IPC, but adds community service as a punishment. The offence remains subject to the ten Exceptions, such as truth for public good and fair comment on public conduct."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"In K.M. Nanavati v State of Maharashtra (1962), the Supreme Court held that the test of 'grave and sudden provocation' is","o":["whether the accused actually felt anger at the time, whatever the cause of it","whether a reasonable man in the accused's position would lose self-control","whether the accused acted within one hour of receiving the provocation","whether the provocation was offered by a close relative of the accused"],"a":1,"e":"The test is objective: whether a reasonable man, placed in the circumstances of the accused, would be so provoked as to lose self-control. The act must be done while the accused is deprived of self-control, before there has been time for his passion to cool."},{"s":"Taxation","q":"Under the Seventh Schedule, the power to levy tax on agricultural income is vested in","o":["Parliament","the GST Council","the State Legislatures","Panchayats and Municipalities alone"],"a":2,"e":"Entry 46 of the State List covers taxes on agricultural income, and Entry 82 of the Union List covers taxes on income 'other than agricultural income'. No State has taxed agricultural income in a substantial way."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Which of the following is a fact of which a court takes judicial notice, so that it need not be proved?","o":["The customs of a particular local community","The signature of a private person on a deed","The foreign law relevant to a case","The laws in force in the territory of India"],"a":3,"e":"Courts take judicial notice of all laws in force in India. Customs, foreign laws and signatures are facts that must be proved by evidence."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"Under the First Schedule of the RFCTLARR Act, 2013, the market value of land in rural areas is multiplied by a factor of up to","o":["three","two","one","five"],"a":1,"e":"The multiplication factor in rural areas is between one and two, depending on the distance from the urban area, as notified by the appropriate Government. The factor in urban areas is one."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under the Special Marriage Act, 1954, a marriage is solemnised in the presence of the Marriage Officer and at least","o":["two witnesses","five witnesses","four witnesses","three witnesses"],"a":3,"e":"Section 12 requires the parties and three witnesses to be present before the Marriage Officer, and the parties to make the declaration prescribed in the Act. No religious ceremony is needed."},{"s":"Administrative Law","q":"In In re Delhi Laws Act, 1912 (1951), the Supreme Court held that the legislature cannot delegate","o":["only the power to levy taxes on income","the power to frame subordinate rules and regulations","its essential legislative functions","any legislative power whatsoever to any authority"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that a legislature may delegate ancillary or subsidiary legislative powers, but must retain essential legislative functions such as laying down the policy and the legislative standard. Excessive delegation is unconstitutional."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 311(1) of the Constitution, a member of a civil service of the Union or a State may be dismissed or removed by","o":["any officer who is senior to him in the same department","the head of his department in every case without exception","the Public Service Commission concerned, on its own motion","an authority not subordinate to the one that appointed him"],"a":3,"e":"Article 311(1) protects civil servants from dismissal or removal by an authority subordinate to the appointing authority. Article 311(2) adds that no one shall be dismissed, removed or reduced in rank without an inquiry in which he has been informed of the charges and given a reasonable opportunity of being heard."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"A 'necessary party' to a suit is one","o":["in whose absence no effective decree can be passed","who is added solely at the defendant's request","whose presence is merely desirable to avoid multiplicity of suits","who is only a witness to the transaction in dispute"],"a":0,"e":"Two tests identify a necessary party: there must be a right to some relief against him in respect of the matter in the suit, and no effective decree can be passed in his absence. A proper party is one whose presence enables the court to decide the questions completely."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Leading questions cannot, without the permission of the court, be asked in","o":["cross-examination by the adverse party","questions on introductory or undisputed matters","examination-in-chief","examination of a witness declared hostile, with the court's leave"],"a":2,"e":"A leading question suggests the answer. It is not allowed in examination-in-chief or re-examination without the court's permission, but is allowed in cross-examination, and on introductory or undisputed matters."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 32 of the Advocates Act, 1961, a court, authority or person may permit a person who is not enrolled as an advocate to appear before it","o":["in a particular case","in all cases for a period of one year","only in civil cases of low value","only on the written consent of the opposite party"],"a":0,"e":"Section 32 empowers a court, authority or person to permit any person not enrolled as an advocate to appear before it in a particular case. The permission is case-specific, not general."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 20 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, an agreement is void where both parties are under a mistake as to","o":["the motives or intentions of the other party to the agreement","the market value of the subject-matter of the agreement","a matter of fact essential to the agreement","a matter of law that is in force in India at the time of agreement"],"a":2,"e":"A mistake as to a matter of fact essential to the agreement, made by both parties, makes the agreement void. A mistake as to Indian law (Section 21), or as to value, does not void the contract."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Under Section 124(1) of the BNS, the minimum punishment for voluntarily causing grievous hurt by use of acid is","o":["imprisonment of not less than seven years, extendable to life, and fine","imprisonment up to ten years, and fine at the court's discretion","imprisonment of not less than five years, extendable to ten years, and fine","imprisonment of not less than ten years, extendable to life, and fine"],"a":3,"e":"Section 124 BNS corresponds to Sections 326A and 326B IPC. Fine must be just and reasonable to meet the medical expenses of the victim, and must be paid to the victim."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"For disobedience of a temporary injunction under Order XXXIX Rule 2A CPC, the court may order the detention of the person in civil prison for a period of up to","o":["six months","one month","three months","one year"],"a":2,"e":"Under Rule 2A, the court may order attachment of the property of the person guilty of disobedience for up to one year, and detention in civil prison for up to three months. If disobedience continues, the property may be sold."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"An easement is distinct from a licence under the Indian Easements Act, 1882 because an easement","o":["does not require a dominant heritage to exist, unlike a licence","is annexed to the dominant heritage and passes with it","is a personal right of the grantee that cannot be transferred","is revocable at the will of the grantor, like a mere permission"],"a":1,"e":"An easement is a right of the owner of a dominant heritage over the servient heritage, which runs with the land. A licence under Section 52 is a personal permission, generally revocable, and does not create an interest in the property."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"The rule that facts forming part of the same transaction are relevant, even though not in issue, is known as","o":["res nullius","res gestae","res ipsa loquitur","res communis"],"a":1,"e":"Section 4 BSA (earlier Section 6 Evidence Act) makes relevant facts that, though not in issue, are so connected with a fact in issue as to form part of the same transaction. Spontaneity and contemporaneity are the key tests."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under the proviso to Section 372 of the CrPC (now Section 413 of the BNSS), a victim has a right to appeal against","o":["only an order granting bail to the accused person during the trial","only an order of conviction passed by a Court of Session after trial","only an order refusing to frame a charge against the accused person","an order of acquittal, conviction for a lesser offence, or inadequate compensation"],"a":3,"e":"The proviso, added in 2009, gives the victim an independent right to appeal against acquittal, conviction for a lesser offence, or imposition of inadequate compensation. In Satya Pal Singh v State of M.P. (2015), the Court held that the appeal lies to the court to which an appeal ordinarily lies against the conviction."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under Section 187 of the BNSS, police custody of an accused may be authorised for a maximum of fifteen days in total, which may be taken","o":["only in a single continuous period, which must fall within the first fifteen days","only in the last fifteen days before the right to default bail arises","in whole or in parts, within the first forty or sixty days of detention","only after the charge-sheet has been filed in the court of the Magistrate"],"a":2,"e":"BNSS allows the 15-day police custody to be availed in whole or in parts during the first 40 days of a 60-day detention period, or the first 60 days of a 90-day detention period. This changes the earlier understanding that it had to be taken in the first fifteen days."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"In law, a retracted confession","o":["may be the basis of conviction if found voluntary and true, though corroboration is ordinarily sought","can never be acted upon by a court as a basis of conviction in any case at all","must be ignored by the court unless the confessor is cross-examined at length","is valid only if it is corroborated by the confession of a co-accused person"],"a":0,"e":"In Pyare Lal Bhargava v State of Rajasthan (1963), the Supreme Court held that a retracted confession may form the basis of conviction if the court is satisfied that it was true and voluntary, though as a rule of prudence the court looks for corroboration."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 20 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, no court can initiate proceedings for contempt after the expiry of","o":["one year from the date on which the contempt is alleged to have been committed","three years from the date on which the contempt is alleged to have been committed","two years from the date on which the contempt is alleged to have been committed","six months from the date on which the contempt is alleged to have been committed"],"a":0,"e":"Section 20 provides a limitation of one year for initiating contempt proceedings. The period is counted from the date of the alleged contempt."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under the provisions of CrPC and BNSS on bar to cognizance after lapse of limitation, the period within which cognizance must be taken of an offence punishable with imprisonment exceeding one year but not exceeding three years is","o":["five years","one year","three years","six months"],"a":2,"e":"The limitation is six months for offences punishable with fine only, one year for imprisonment up to one year, and three years for imprisonment exceeding one year and up to three years. There is no limitation for offences punishable with more than three years."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Under Section 23 of the Advocates Act, 1961, the right of pre-audience in the Supreme Court belongs first to","o":["a Senior Advocate designated by the Court","the Solicitor General of India","the Advocate General of a State","the Attorney General of India"],"a":3,"e":"Section 23 sets the order of pre-audience: the Attorney General, the Solicitor General, Additional Solicitors General, Advocates General of States, Senior Advocates, and then other advocates."},{"s":"Company Law","q":"Under the Companies Act, 2013, the minimum number of members required to form a public company is","o":["two","seven","three","five"],"a":1,"e":"A public company needs at least seven members, a private company two, and a One Person Company one. A public company must also have at least three directors."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Which of the following is a ground on which a marriage is VOIDABLE under Section 12 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955?","o":["The parties are sapindas of each other, without a custom permitting it","Either party had a living spouse at the time the marriage was solemnised","The respondent was pregnant by another person at the time of marriage","The parties are within the degrees of prohibited relationship, without a custom permitting it"],"a":2,"e":"Section 12 provides for annulment on impotence, unsound mind, consent obtained by force or fraud, and pregnancy of the respondent by another person at the time of marriage. Bigamy, prohibited relationship and sapinda relationship make a marriage void under Section 11."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Non-registration of a firm under Section 69 of the Indian Partnership Act, 1932 bars","o":["a suit by the firm against a third party to enforce a contractual right","a suit by a partner for the dissolution of the firm and settlement of accounts","a suit for accounts of a firm which has already been dissolved","a suit by a third party against the firm to recover a debt owed to him"],"a":0,"e":"Section 69 bars suits by the firm, or by its partners on its behalf, against third parties to enforce a contractual right. It does not bar suits against the firm, suits for dissolution or accounts, or claims of set-off."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"In Novartis AG v Union of India (2013), the Supreme Court held that Section 3(d) of the Patents Act, 1970 requires, for a new form of a known substance, proof of","o":["only that the new form is capable of industrial application","enhanced therapeutic efficacy over the known substance","only that a new manufacturing process has been devised","only that the new form is novel and not previously known"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that, for pharmaceutical substances, 'efficacy' means therapeutic efficacy, and the patentee must show a significant enhancement. The Court rejected the patent claim for the beta crystalline form of imatinib mesylate (Glivec)."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"Under Section 105 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), the burden of proving that a case falls within any General Exception in the penal law lies on","o":["the accused who claims the benefit of the exception","the prosecution in all cases, without exception","the Magistrate trying the case, on his own motion","the complainant, along with the investigating officer"],"a":0,"e":"The court must presume the absence of circumstances bringing the case within an exception. The accused has to prove it, but only on the standard of preponderance of probability, not beyond reasonable doubt."},{"s":"Contract, Specific Relief & Sale of Goods","q":"Under Section 74 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, where a contract names a sum payable on breach, the party complaining of breach is entitled to","o":["only the actual loss proved, even if that is less than the sum named","reasonable compensation not exceeding the sum named, whether or not actual loss is proved","double the amount named, if the breach was wilful on the part of the party","the full amount named in all cases, without any reduction by the court"],"a":1,"e":"In Fateh Chand v Balkishan Dass (1963), the Supreme Court held that Section 74 applies to both liquidated damages and penalties. The sum named is the maximum, and the court awards reasonable compensation. It is not necessary to prove actual loss unless the damage is difficult to assess."},{"s":"Intellectual Property Law","q":"Under Section 57 of the Copyright Act, 1957, the moral rights of authorship (the right to claim authorship and to restrain distortion) belong to","o":["the author, even after assignment of the copyright","the publisher of the work in all cases","the assignee of the copyright after the assignment","the Registrar of Copyrights on behalf of the author"],"a":0,"e":"Moral rights, or the rights of 'paternity' and 'integrity', are independent of the author's copyright and remain with the author even after the copyright is assigned."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT a ground for proclaiming a national emergency under Article 352 of the Constitution as amended by the 44th Amendment?","o":["War","Armed rebellion","Internal disturbance","External aggression"],"a":2,"e":"The 44th Amendment replaced 'internal disturbance' with 'armed rebellion'. A proclamation must also be based on the written recommendation of the Union Cabinet, and be approved by Parliament within one month."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Which of the following is NOT criminal contempt under Section 2(c) of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971?","o":["Obstructing the administration of justice in any other manner","Wilful disobedience of a judgment, decree or order of a court","Prejudicing or interfering with due course of a judicial proceeding","Scandalising or lowering the authority of any court"],"a":1,"e":"Wilful disobedience of a court's judgment, decree, direction, order or writ is civil contempt under Section 2(b). Criminal contempt under Section 2(c) covers scandalising the court, prejudicing judicial proceedings, and obstructing the administration of justice."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT a Class I heir of a Hindu male dying intestate under the Hindu Succession Act, 1956?","o":["Widow","Father","Mother","Daughter"],"a":1,"e":"Class I heirs include son, daughter, widow, mother, and the specified descendants of predeceased sons and daughters. The father is a Class II heir (Entry I), who inherits only if there is no Class I heir."},{"s":"Labour Law","q":"Under the Code on Social Security, 2020, the contribution payable by an aggregator towards the social security of gig and platform workers is","o":["one to two per cent of annual turnover, capped at five per cent of amounts paid to such workers","a fixed fee per worker per year, irrespective of the turnover of the aggregator","five to ten per cent of annual turnover, with no cap of any kind on the amount","one to two per cent of annual profits, with no cap on the amount payable"],"a":0,"e":"The Code recognises gig and platform workers for the first time and allows the Central Government to notify the contribution rate for aggregators within these limits."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 9 of the CPC, civil courts have jurisdiction to try all suits of a civil nature, except suits of which their cognizance is","o":["barred only by the Constitution of India","expressly barred only, and never by implication","barred only by an order of the High Court","expressly or impliedly barred"],"a":3,"e":"The bar may be express, or may be implied from the scheme of a statute that provides a complete remedy and a special forum. Exclusion of civil court jurisdiction is not readily inferred (Dhulabhai v State of M.P.)."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"In R.D. Saxena v Balram Prasad Sharma (2000), the Supreme Court held that an advocate","o":["may retain a client's papers only with the written permission of the court","may retain a client's papers until his fees have been paid in full by the client","may retain a client's papers only for a limited period of about three months","has no lien over a client's papers and cannot retain them for unpaid fees"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that an advocate's refusal to return the client's papers on termination of engagement, on the ground of unpaid fees, is professional misconduct. The advocate may sue for the fees but has no right of lien over the papers."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"Under Muslim law, the iddat period of a widow is","o":["four months only, counted from the date of the death","four months and ten days","three menstrual courses after the husband's death","three months from the date of the husband's death"],"a":1,"e":"A widow observes iddat for four months and ten days after her husband's death. If she is pregnant, the iddat continues until delivery if that is later. A divorced woman who is subject to menstruation observes three courses."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"Under Section 58 CPC, a judgment-debtor may be detained in civil prison for a decree for payment of money exceeding Rs. 5,000 for a period not exceeding","o":["six months","six weeks","one year","three months"],"a":3,"e":"Detention is up to three months where the decree is for over Rs. 5,000, six weeks where it exceeds Rs. 2,000 but not Rs. 5,000, and no detention where it does not exceed Rs. 2,000."},{"s":"Criminal Procedure (BNSS)","q":"Under the Chapter on plea bargaining in the BNSS, an application for plea bargaining must be filed within","o":["fifteen days from the date of framing of charge","thirty days from the date of framing of charge","forty-five days from the date of framing of charge","sixty days from the date of framing of charge"],"a":1,"e":"The BNSS adds a 30-day time limit from the framing of charge. As earlier, plea bargaining is unavailable for offences punishable with death, life imprisonment or more than seven years, socio-economic offences, and offences against women or children under fourteen."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"Under Article 254(2), a State law on a Concurrent List subject that is repugnant to an earlier Union law prevails in that State if it","o":["has been reserved for and has received the assent of the President","has been passed by a two-thirds majority in the State Legislature","has received the assent of the Governor after a resolution of the Council of States","has been approved by the Supreme Court on a reference"],"a":0,"e":"Article 254(2) allows a State law, repugnant to an earlier Union law, to prevail in that State if it has been reserved for the President's consideration and received his assent. Parliament may still later enact a law on the same matter, which can override the State law."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Section 152 of the BNS, which replaced the offence of sedition under Section 124A of the IPC, penalises","o":["acts endangering the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India","waging war against the Government of India","any criticism of Government policy made through speech or writing","exciting disaffection towards the Government established by law"],"a":0,"e":"Section 152 BNS penalises, with life imprisonment or up to seven years and fine, acts such as secession, armed rebellion, subversive activities or encouraging separatist feelings. It does not use the word 'sedition'. Waging war is dealt with separately under Section 147."},{"s":"Property Law","q":"Under Section 106 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a lease of immovable property for non-agricultural purposes from month to month is terminable by","o":["seven days' notice in writing expiring at the end of a month of the tenancy","thirty days' notice in writing expiring at the end of a month of the tenancy","fifteen days' notice in writing expiring at the end of a month of the tenancy","one month's notice in writing expiring at the end of the year of tenancy"],"a":2,"e":"Unless a contract or local law provides otherwise, a lease for agricultural or manufacturing purposes is deemed from year to year, terminable by six months' notice. Other leases from month to month require fifteen days' notice. The notice must be in writing."},{"s":"Evidence (BSA)","q":"A confession by a co-accused, taken into consideration under Section 30 of the Evidence Act (and its corresponding BSA provision), is","o":["admissible only if the co-accused later turns approver and gives evidence","not substantive evidence, and can only lend assurance to other evidence","admissible only against the person who made the confession and no one else","substantive evidence on which alone a conviction can safely be based"],"a":1,"e":"In Kashmira Singh v State of M.P. (1952), the Supreme Court held that a co-accused's confession is a very weak type of evidence. It cannot be made the foundation of a conviction, and can be used only to lend assurance to other evidence."},{"s":"Family Law","q":"In Saroj Rani v Sudarshan Kumar Chadha (1984), the Supreme Court","o":["upheld the constitutional validity of restitution of conjugal rights under Section 9 of the HMA","struck down Section 9 of the HMA as violating Articles 14 and 21 of the Constitution","held that Section 9 violates the wife's right to privacy and human dignity","held that restitution of conjugal rights is available only to the wife and not the husband"],"a":0,"e":"The Court overruled the Andhra Pradesh High Court's decision in T. Sareetha v T. Venkata Subbaiah, which had struck down the remedy. It held that Section 9 serves a social purpose as a means to preserve the marriage."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS)","q":"Abetment of suicide under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 is punishable under which Section?","o":["Section 106","Section 108","Section 109","Section 105"],"a":1,"e":"Section 108 BNS corresponds to Section 306 IPC. Section 107 deals with abetment of suicide of a child or a person of unsound mind. Section 106 deals with death by negligence and Section 109 with attempt to murder."},{"s":"Interpretation of Statutes","q":"The mischief rule of statutory interpretation originated in","o":["Pepper v Hart (1993)","Sussex Peerage Case (1844)","Heydon's Case (1584)","Grey v Pearson (1857)"],"a":2,"e":"Heydon's Case directs the court to consider the common law before the Act, the mischief for which the common law did not provide, the remedy provided, and the true reason for the remedy. The Sussex Peerage Case is associated with the literal rule and Grey v Pearson with the golden rule."},{"s":"Professional Ethics & Advocates Act","q":"Which of the following is an ex officio member of the Bar Council of India under Section 4 of the Advocates Act, 1961?","o":["The Law Secretary to the Government of India","The Chairman of the Law Commission of India","The Chief Justice of India","The Attorney General of India"],"a":3,"e":"The BCI consists of the Attorney General of India and the Solicitor General of India as ex officio members, and one member elected by each State Bar Council from among its members."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","q":"The doctrine of 'essential religious practices' under Article 25 originated in which decision?","o":["Indian Young Lawyers Association v State of Kerala (2018), the Sabarimala case","Bijoe Emmanuel v State of Kerala (1986), the national anthem case","Aruna Roy v Union of India (2002), on religious education in schools","Commissioner, Hindu Religious Endowments v Shirur Mutt (1954)"],"a":3,"e":"Shirur Mutt held that what constitutes an essential part of a religion is to be determined with reference to its own doctrines and tenets. Later decisions, including the Sabarimala case, applied the doctrine."},{"s":"Civil Procedure (CPC)","q":"The principle of restitution under Section 144 CPC is based on the maxim","o":["actus curiae neminem gravabit","ubi jus ibi remedium, where there is a right there is a remedy","audi alteram partem, hear the other side","res ipsa loquitur, the thing speaks for itself"],"a":0,"e":"'An act of the court shall prejudice no one.' Where a decree is varied or reversed, the court must place the parties in the position they would have occupied but for the decree, including restitution of benefits received under it."},{"s":"Land Acquisition Law","q":"Under the RFCTLARR Act, 2013, the Social Impact Assessment study must be completed within","o":["three months from the date of its commencement","twelve months from the date of its commencement","six months from the date of its commencement","nine months from the date of its commencement"],"a":2,"e":"Under Section 4, the appropriate Government must carry out a Social Impact Assessment, which must be completed within six months of its commencement. The report is then appraised by an Expert Group."}]};
